1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,732
Thursday, 18 June 2020.

2
00:00:01,732 --> 00:00:05,948
Ten in the morning, Aschheim, on the eastern edge of Munich.

3
00:00:05,948 --> 00:00:10,546
Wirecard AG, a member of Germany's DAX 30, publishes a short statement.

4
00:00:10,546 --> 00:00:22,043
Its auditor, EY, cannot sign off on the 2019 accounts, because it has been unable to obtain sufficient evidence that €1.9 billion in cash held in trust accounts actually exists.

5
00:00:22,043 --> 00:00:30,857
The chief executive, Markus Braun, adds that it "cannot be ruled out" that Wirecard has been "the victim of fraud of considerable proportions".

6
00:00:30,857 --> 00:00:35,072
The share price loses roughly two-thirds of its value before lunch.

7
00:00:35,072 --> 00:00:44,653
Within a week the company will be insolvent, the chief operating officer will have vanished, and Germany's financial regulator will have some explaining to do.

8
00:00:45,053 --> 00:00:49,094
That line tells most of the story in one shape.

9
00:00:49,094 --> 00:00:57,985
Wirecard was founded in 1999, survived the dot-com crash processing payments for online gambling and pornography, and reinvented itself as a "fintech".

10
00:00:57,985 --> 00:01:04,046
By September 2018 it was worth about €24 billion at a share price near €191.

11
00:01:04,046 --> 00:01:11,320
Then a downward staircase: each step is a Financial Times article, a regulator's intervention, or an auditor's report.

12
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:14,553
The final vertical drop is 18 June 2020.

13
00:01:14,553 --> 00:01:18,190
Within weeks the shares traded for around a euro.

14
00:01:18,190 --> 00:01:28,698
Note the rally in mid-2019: that is what happens when a regulator bans betting against a company and files criminal complaints against the people investigating it.

15
00:01:28,698 --> 00:01:31,122
We will come back to that.

16
00:01:31,522 --> 00:01:40,744
Markus Braun, an Austrian former KPMG consultant, took over as chief executive in 2002 and became the largest shareholder, holding around seven percent.

17
00:01:40,744 --> 00:01:48,763
He wore black turtlenecks and spoke in the register of Silicon Valley, about artificial intelligence and "the digitalisation of everything".

18
00:01:48,763 --> 00:01:54,377
Jan Marsalek, also Austrian, joined at 20 and was chief operating officer from 2010.

19
00:01:54,377 --> 00:02:08,009
He ran the operational side, especially Asia, and the relationships with the "third-party acquirers" in Dubai, Manila and Singapore that would later turn out to be where most of the profit supposedly came from.

20
00:02:08,009 --> 00:02:09,613
Braun sold the vision.

21
00:02:09,613 --> 00:02:11,217
Marsalek supplied the numbers.

22
00:02:11,217 --> 00:02:15,627
Between them sat a supervisory board that asked remarkably few questions.

23
00:02:16,027 --> 00:02:22,518
The timeline runs from a Munich start-up to a fugitive in Moscow, but the interesting part is the middle.

24
00:02:22,518 --> 00:02:32,424
Between the FT's first sceptical articles in 2015 and the collapse in 2020 lie five years in which the questions were public, specific and, as it turned out, correct.

25
00:02:32,424 --> 00:02:35,499
What is notable is not that a fraud existed.

26
00:02:35,499 --> 00:02:36,182
Frauds exist.

27
00:02:36,182 --> 00:02:43,014
What is notable is how many institutions with the power to look, chose instead to look at the people asking.

28
00:02:43,414 --> 00:02:53,366
On 24 September 2018, Wirecard entered the DAX, Germany's index of thirty blue chips, taking the place of Commerzbank, a bank founded in 1870.

29
00:02:53,366 --> 00:03:00,415
The symbolism was irresistible and everybody used it: the old lender out, the young payments platform in.

30
00:03:00,415 --> 00:03:04,977
At its peak, Wirecard's market value exceeded that of Deutsche Bank.

31
00:03:04,977 --> 00:03:11,611
Germany, which had watched the internet economy happen mostly elsewhere, finally had a home-grown technology champion.

32
00:03:11,611 --> 00:03:12,855
This mattered later.

33
00:03:12,855 --> 00:03:21,563
Once a company becomes a national story, criticism of it starts to look, to certain officials, like criticism of the nation.

34
00:03:21,563 --> 00:03:23,637
Wirecard's management leaned into that.

35
00:03:23,637 --> 00:03:28,613
Sceptics were not analysts with a different view; they were foreign attackers.

36
00:03:29,013 --> 00:03:36,953
The Financial Times had been publishing sceptical pieces since April 2015, in a series its Alphaville blog called "House of Wirecard".

37
00:03:36,953 --> 00:03:46,405
Reporter Dan McCrum's early questions were about the accounts: gaps between reported cash and reported profit, and Asian acquisitions at prices that made little sense.

38
00:03:46,405 --> 00:03:48,674
The response was not a rebuttal.

39
00:03:48,674 --> 00:03:57,748
It was legal threats, surveillance and, as McCrum later documented in his book, a sustained effort to paint him as a co-conspirator of short-sellers.

40
00:03:57,748 --> 00:04:01,530
Wirecard hired law firms and, it emerged, private intelligence operators.

41
00:04:01,530 --> 00:04:12,116
This is worth stating plainly: the reporting was later shown to be substantially accurate, and the company's response to it was, in the end, part of the fraud.

42
00:04:12,516 --> 00:04:17,165
The geography of Wirecard is the geography of distance from its auditors.

43
00:04:17,165 --> 00:04:19,876
Munich for the headquarters and the listing.

44
00:04:19,876 --> 00:04:27,236
Dublin and Dubai for subsidiaries whose profits, according to internal documents the FT published in October 2019, appeared inflated.

45
00:04:27,236 --> 00:04:39,631
Singapore for the regional office where, in January 2019, the FT reported a senior executive was suspected of forging and backdating contracts, citing an internal legal review the company had sat on.

46
00:04:39,631 --> 00:04:43,892
Manila for the two banks supposedly holding €1.9 billion in escrow.

47
00:04:43,892 --> 00:04:46,991
And Vienna and Moscow for the last chapter.

48
00:04:46,991 --> 00:04:48,928
The pattern is not subtle.

49
00:04:48,928 --> 00:04:50,865
Revenue was booked in Germany.

50
00:04:50,865 --> 00:04:58,612
Its supposed origins were always somewhere an auditor would need a visa and a great deal of patience to reach.

51
00:04:59,012 --> 00:05:02,870
The mechanism deserves a minute, because it is not complicated.

52
00:05:02,870 --> 00:05:12,901
In markets where Wirecard lacked a licence, it said it processed payments through third-party partners, who kept the customer money in trust accounts and remitted commissions.

53
00:05:12,901 --> 00:05:18,302
Wirecard reported those commissions as revenue and the trust balances as its own cash.

54
00:05:18,302 --> 00:05:27,176
KPMG's later investigation would find that this segment supplied, in the years 2016 to 2018, roughly the entire operating profit of the group.

55
00:05:27,176 --> 00:05:31,419
So the real business, the licensed European card processing, was thin.

56
00:05:31,419 --> 00:05:45,308
The profitable business was the part nobody could verify: partners with a handful of staff, in jurisdictions far away, holding cash in accounts that Wirecard's own auditor accepted on the strength of confirmations from those partners.

57
00:05:45,708 --> 00:05:51,022
Now the part that makes this a German story rather than a generic one.

58
00:05:51,022 --> 00:05:58,992
On 30 January 2019 the FT reported the Singapore forgery investigation; shares fell by around a quarter over the following days.

59
00:05:58,992 --> 00:06:13,415
On 18 February the Federal Financial Supervisory Authority, BaFin, imposed a two-month ban on new short positions in Wirecard shares, the first time it had ever done so for a single company, citing a threat to market confidence.

60
00:06:13,415 --> 00:06:22,144
On 16 April, BaFin filed a criminal complaint with Munich prosecutors against Dan McCrum and his colleague Stefania Palma, alleging possible market manipulation.

61
00:06:22,144 --> 00:06:29,355
The regulator, in other words, had examined a fraud allegation and decided to investigate the people who made it.

62
00:06:29,755 --> 00:06:33,246
Here is our view, and we will support it.

63
00:06:33,246 --> 00:06:38,289
BaFin's conduct in 2019 was not merely a misjudgment; it was regulatory nationalism.

64
00:06:38,289 --> 00:06:47,210
The evidence: BaFin had classified Wirecard as a technology company rather than a financial group, so it supervised only the small bank subsidiary.

65
00:06:47,210 --> 00:06:56,907
It received a whistleblower tip in early 2019 and referred the accounts to the private accounting watchdog FREP, which assigned, famously, a single part-time examiner.

66
00:06:56,907 --> 00:07:03,113
When the FT reported, BaFin's instinct was to defend the share price and pursue the foreigners.

67
00:07:03,113 --> 00:07:10,094
Its president, Felix Hufeld, later called the affair a "complete disaster" and was pushed out in January 2021.

68
00:07:10,094 --> 00:07:14,361
Munich prosecutors dropped the case against the journalists in September 2020.

69
00:07:14,361 --> 00:07:18,240
By then there was no share price left to protect.

70
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:32,147
In October 2019 the FT published internal Wirecard documents suggesting that profits at units in Dubai and Dublin had been fraudulently inflated, and that customers named in the accounts had, in some cases, never heard of the arrangements.

71
00:07:32,147 --> 00:07:40,678
Wirecard's response was to appoint KPMG to conduct an independent special audit, which it advertised as the thing that would finally silence the sceptics.

72
00:07:40,678 --> 00:07:49,564
The company plainly expected a clean bill of health; so, presumably, did Softbank, which had put in €900 million through a convertible bond that spring.

73
00:07:49,564 --> 00:07:59,161
In hindsight, engaging a second Big Four firm to look at what the first had been signing off for a decade was the mistake that ended it.

74
00:07:59,561 --> 00:08:06,457
The KPMG report arrived on 28 April 2020, months late, and it was not what the company had ordered.

75
00:08:06,457 --> 00:08:19,524
KPMG said it could not confirm that the third-party acquirer revenues for 2016 through 2018 were genuine, because it had been unable to obtain the documents, or in some cases the cooperation, needed to check them.

76
00:08:19,524 --> 00:08:21,702
It cited "obstacles to the investigation".

77
00:08:21,702 --> 00:08:29,325
Wirecard published it under a press release saying no evidence of balance-sheet manipulation had been found, which was a creative reading.

78
00:08:29,325 --> 00:08:32,591
The shares fell by around a quarter that day.

79
00:08:32,591 --> 00:08:34,769
Braun insisted everything was in order.

80
00:08:34,769 --> 00:08:42,029
Marsalek, whose department generated most of the unanswerable questions, was quietly stripped of some duties in May, though not removed.

81
00:08:42,429 --> 00:08:45,437
Which brings us back to the June morning.

82
00:08:45,437 --> 00:08:52,958
EY, which had audited Wirecard since 2009 and had issued unqualified opinions every year, refused to sign the 2019 accounts.

83
00:08:52,958 --> 00:08:57,095
The trust-account balances at two Philippine banks could not be confirmed.

84
00:08:57,095 --> 00:09:00,856
Braun resigned the next day; Marsalek was suspended, then dismissed.

85
00:09:00,856 --> 00:09:11,761
Bank of the Philippine Islands and BDO Unibank said the confirmation documents bearing their names were forged; the Philippine central bank said the money had never entered the country.

86
00:09:11,761 --> 00:09:16,650
On 22 June the company admitted the €1.9 billion "probably does not exist".

87
00:09:16,650 --> 00:09:23,043
Braun was arrested that evening in Munich, released on €5 million bail, and re-arrested a month later.

88
00:09:23,043 --> 00:09:28,684
On 25 June, Wirecard filed for insolvency, the first DAX member ever to do so.

89
00:09:29,084 --> 00:09:31,698
Jan Marsalek's exit is its own genre.

90
00:09:31,698 --> 00:09:37,673
Suspended on 18 June, he told colleagues he was flying to Manila to locate the money.

91
00:09:37,673 --> 00:09:57,464
Instead, according to the joint investigation by Der Spiegel, ZDF, Der Standard and The Insider published in March 2024, he was driven to a small airfield at Bad Vöslau, south of Vienna, on the night of 18 to 19 June, and flown by private jet to Minsk, then taken by car into Russia.

92
00:09:57,464 --> 00:10:03,439
Austrian officials, including a former intelligence officer, have since been investigated over the help he received.

93
00:10:03,439 --> 00:10:07,920
Manila immigration records were doctored to show him arriving in the Philippines.

94
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:10,534
He has not been seen publicly since.

95
00:10:10,534 --> 00:10:12,028
Moscow does not extradite.

96
00:10:12,428 --> 00:10:14,990
The 2024 reporting reframed the whole affair.

97
00:10:14,990 --> 00:10:32,924
It alleged that Marsalek had been recruited by a GRU handler in 2014, had worked for Russian services for the better part of a decade while running the operations of a DAX company, and now lives in Moscow under the borrowed identity of a provincial Orthodox priest, passport included.

98
00:10:32,924 --> 00:10:46,466
In London, six Bulgarian nationals were convicted at the Old Bailey of running a Russian surveillance operation from a Norfolk guesthouse on instructions relayed by Marsalek; sentences handed down in May 2025 totalled more than fifty years.

99
00:10:46,466 --> 00:10:53,054
Whether the espionage was funded from Wirecard, or Wirecard was cover for it, no court has yet answered.

100
00:10:53,054 --> 00:10:56,713
It does rather change what "third-party acquirer" might have meant.

101
00:10:57,113 --> 00:11:03,376
The criminal trial opened on 8 December 2022 in a courtroom beneath Munich's Stadelheim prison.

102
00:11:03,376 --> 00:11:17,154
Braun is charged with commercial gang fraud, falsifying the 2016 to 2018 accounts, market manipulation and breach of trust; prosecutors say roughly €3 billion in revenue was fabricated to keep bank credit flowing.

103
00:11:17,154 --> 00:11:22,164
Oliver Bellenhaus, who ran the Dubai unit, confessed and testifies against him.

104
00:11:22,164 --> 00:11:28,009
Braun says he was the victim of a gang run by Marsalek and Bellenhaus.

105
00:11:28,009 --> 00:11:35,942
He has been on remand since July 2020, six years as of this recording, unusually long by German standards.

106
00:11:35,942 --> 00:11:42,622
In February 2025 the indictment was cut from 43 counts to ten to speed things up.

107
00:11:42,622 --> 00:11:52,225
As of July 2026, after more than 270 hearing days, closing arguments are scheduled from 19 October 2026, with a verdict to follow.

108
00:11:52,625 --> 00:11:55,225
The consequences have arrived in instalments.

109
00:11:55,225 --> 00:12:10,397
Germany's audit watchdog APAS fined EY €500,000 in April 2023 and barred it from taking on new audits of public-interest companies for two years, the heaviest sanction it had ever imposed, and reprimanded individual partners.

110
00:12:10,397 --> 00:12:32,937
In September 2024, Munich Regional Court I ordered Braun, Marsalek and former chief accountant Stephan von Erffa to pay €140 million to the insolvency administrator over an unsecured €100 million loan and an unchecked bond purchase; that judgment is under appeal, and collecting from a defendant in Moscow is a theoretical exercise.

111
00:12:32,937 --> 00:12:35,538
Shareholders' claims were ranked behind creditors.

112
00:12:35,538 --> 00:12:39,006
BaFin's leadership was replaced and its powers expanded.

113
00:12:39,006 --> 00:12:45,074
EY faces civil claims running to billions, in proceedings that will outlast this channel.

114
00:12:45,474 --> 00:12:47,020
Our editorial position, restated.

115
00:12:47,020 --> 00:12:50,111
Wirecard was a fraud designed by its perpetrators.

116
00:12:50,111 --> 00:12:59,770
But its longevity was enabled by a regulatory culture that treated a listed company as something to defend and its critics as something to police.

117
00:12:59,770 --> 00:13:14,837
When a regulator's first reflex on hearing a credible fraud allegation is to protect the share price and file a complaint against foreign journalists, that regulator has stopped functioning as a regulator and started functioning as an investor-relations department.

118
00:13:14,837 --> 00:13:19,860
Germany has since reformed BaFin's mandate and given it direct forensic audit powers.

119
00:13:19,860 --> 00:13:21,019
That is welcome.

120
00:13:21,019 --> 00:13:25,655
It is also an admission of exactly what was missing in 2019.

121
00:13:26,055 --> 00:13:31,092
Lest anyone in Copenhagen or Stockholm feel superior, a note for a future episode.

122
00:13:31,092 --> 00:13:48,361
Around the same years, Danske Bank's Estonian branch was moving roughly €200 billion of suspicious money and Swedbank's Baltic operations were doing a smaller but similar trade, and in both cases the local supervisors were slow, polite and largely absent until journalists and a whistleblower did the work.

123
00:13:48,361 --> 00:13:54,117
The Nordic version of the disease was not aggressive nationalism but a kind of trusting quietness.

124
00:13:54,117 --> 00:13:59,874
The pathogen is the same: a supervisor that assumes its national institutions are the good ones.

125
00:13:59,874 --> 00:14:01,673
We will get to Danske.

126
00:14:01,673 --> 00:14:07,069
It is a longer story than this one, and the money, unlike Wirecard's, was real.

127
00:14:07,469 --> 00:14:27,944
Everything in this episode is drawn from the public record: the Financial Times' reporting from 2015 onward, BaFin's own decrees, the KPMG special audit report, Wirecard's ad hoc disclosures, court reporting from Munich and London, and the 2024 Spiegel, ZDF, Standard and Insider investigation into Marsalek.

128
00:14:27,944 --> 00:14:31,950
Where a number is approximate, the script says so.

129
00:14:31,950 --> 00:14:36,401
Full numbered sources with URLs are in the description below.

130
00:14:36,801 --> 00:14:42,970
That is the Wirecard file, as it stands in August 2026; we will update when the Munich court rules.

131
00:14:42,970 --> 00:14:50,762
If this was useful, subscribing helps the channel more than you would think, and the comments are open for corrections, which we do read.

132
00:14:50,762 --> 00:15:00,177
Next time, something less criminal and arguably more painful: Nokia, and how the company that put a phone in every pocket managed to hand the pocket to someone else.

133
00:15:00,177 --> 00:15:02,450
Thank you for watching The Nordic Ledger.

